    {
      "beat_id": "B01",
      "act": "PROBLEM — why care",
      "role_note": "the problem, the stakes — BEFORE the CLI",
      "narration_text": "A legal team uses an LLM to answer questions about statutes of limitation. They test one phrasing, get a correct answer, and deploy. But the same question, asked ten different ways by different users, generates different time periods. Some are wrong. The model's answer depends on how the question is framed — not just what it asks. This is prompt sensitivity: semantic equivalence does not guarantee output stability. If your application depends on consistent answers to consistent questions, you need to measure this before you ship.",
      "visual_intent": "title card: '10 phrasings — how many answers?'; PROBLEM beat, no terminal",
      "shot": {
        "type": "GRAPHIC",
        "source": "remotion",
        "motion": "fade",
        "remotion": {
          "pattern": "ClaudeComposerAsk",
          "props": {
            "command": "10 phrasings — how many answers?",
            "topic": "COMPUTATIONAL SKEPTICISM",
            "segment": "Prompt Sensitivity, taken apart.",
            "greeting": "",
            "runningText": "framing the problem…",
            "output": [],
            "folderLabel": "@NikBearBrown",
            "modelLabel": "Fable 5",
            "effortLabel": "High"
          },
          "rendered": {
            "out": "media/B01.mp4",
            "at": ""
          }
        }
      },
      "estimated_duration_s": 16,
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B01.mp3",
      "actual_duration_s": 28.35,
      "build": {
        "status": "VIDEO",
        "src": "media/B01.mp4",
        "filled_by": "media",
        "at": "2026-07-19T17:42:10"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B02",
      "act": "ASK",
      "role_note": "terminal (Onda) — the command typed",
      "narration_text": "In the terminal: ask Claude to write a Python script using the Anthropic SDK. Send ten semantic variants of a statute-of-limitations question to claude-three-five-haiku. Extract the stated time period from each response. Build a ten-by-ten agreement matrix showing which pairs agree. Print the overall agreement rate.",
      "shot": {
        "type": "GRAPHIC",
        "source": "remotion",
        "motion": "fade",
        "remotion": {
          "pattern": "ClaudeComposerAsk",
          "props": {
            "command": "claude \"Write a Python script using Anthropic SDK:\nsend 10 semantic variants of:\n  'What is the statute of limitations for a\n   personal injury claim in the US?'\nto claude-3-5-haiku.\nExtract stated time period from each.\nPrint a 10x10 agreement matrix.\nPrint agreement rate.\"",
            "topic": "COMPUTATIONAL SKEPTICISM — PROMPT SENSITIVITY",
            "segment": "LLM PROMPT SENSITIVITY TESTER",
            "greeting": "",
            "runningText": "generating prompt sensitivity tester…",
            "output": [],
            "folderLabel": "@NikBearBrown",
            "modelLabel": "Fable 5",
            "effortLabel": "High"
          },
          "rendered": {
            "out": "media/B02.mp4",
            "at": ""
          }
        }
      },
      "estimated_duration_s": 14,
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B02.mp3",
      "actual_duration_s": 17.49,
      "build": {
        "status": "VIDEO",
        "src": "media/B02.mp4",
        "filled_by": "media",
        "at": "2026-07-19T17:42:10"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B03",
      "act": "CODE",
      "role_note": "terminal (Onda) — read the code before running",
      "narration_text": "Read the key line. Agreement index i j equals one if extract underscore years of response i equals extract underscore years of response j, else zero. The agreement matrix is a ten-by-ten grid of those comparisons. The diagonal is always one — a response agrees with itself. Everything off the diagonal is the measurement of semantic stability.",
      "shot": {
        "type": "GRAPHIC",
        "source": "remotion",
        "motion": "fade",
        "remotion": {
          "pattern": "ClaudeCodeBeat",
          "props": {
            "title": "prompt_sensitivity.py",
            "code": "# prompt_sensitivity.py  —  Claude Code output\nimport re, anthropic\n\ndef extract_years(text):\n    \"\"\"Extract first digit-year mention from response.\"\"\"\n    match = re.search(r'(\\d+)\\s*(?:year|yr)', text, re.I)\n    return match.group(1) if match else None\n\n# Build 10x10 agreement matrix\nagreement = [[0]*10 for _ in range(10)]\nfor i in range(10):\n    for j in range(10):\n        # 1 if responses i and j state the same time period\n        agreement[i][j] = 1 if (\n            extract_years(responses[i]) ==\n            extract_years(responses[j])\n        ) else 0\n\n# Agreement rate = off-diagonal 1s / (10*9)\noff_diag = sum(agreement[i][j] for i in range(10)\n               for j in range(10) if i != j)\nagreement_rate = off_diag / 90",
            "sparkLine": "LLM PROMPT SENSITIVITY TESTER"
          },
          "rendered": {
            "out": "media/B03.mp4",
            "at": ""
          }
        }
      },
      "estimated_duration_s": 14,
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B03.mp3",
      "actual_duration_s": 19.88,
      "build": {
        "status": "VIDEO",
        "src": "media/B03.mp4",
        "filled_by": "media",
        "at": "2026-07-19T17:42:10"
      }
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B04",
      "act": "OUTPUT — run",
      "role_note": "SLATE — screen-recording terminal output — human fill with live API run",
      "narration_text": "Run it against the live API. You will see the agreement matrix and the rate. If the rate is below ninety percent for a legally consequential question, the model is not reliable as deployed — its answer depends on phrasing, not just content.",
      "visual_intent": "HUMAN FILL: screen-recording of prompt_sensitivity.py running — show 10x10 agreement matrix (1s and 0s), extracted time periods per variant, and final agreement rate; highlight disagreeing cells in red",
